  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 2,
    "lesson_title": "Finding Output with $Y(j\\omega)=X(j\\omega)H(j\\omega)$",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute the time-domain output of an LTI system by forming $Y(j\\omega)=X(j\\omega)H(j\\omega)$, simplifying the product, and applying the inverse Fourier transform.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Finding Output with $Y(j\\omega)=X(j\\omega)H(j\\omega)$",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Finding Output with $Y(j\\omega)=X(j\\omega)H(j\\omega)$",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When an LTI system is described in the frequency domain, the output is found by staying in $j\\omega$ until the output spectrum is formed. If the input spectrum is $X(j\\omega)$ and the system response is $H(j\\omega)$, then the output spectrum is $Y(j\\omega)=X(j\\omega)H(j\\omega)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(j\\omega)=X(j\\omega)H(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "$H(j\\omega)$ comes from the impulse response $h(t)$, so the system is already represented by a transform pair. The only new work is to identify the two factors, multiply them, and simplify the product only as much as needed.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\mathcal{F}^{-1}\\{Y(j\\omega)\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The full procedure is always the same: read off $X(j\\omega)$ and $H(j\\omega)$, form $Y(j\\omega)$, simplify the product if that makes the inverse transform easier, and then apply the inverse Fourier transform to recover $y(t)$. Keep the first pass small so the theorem attaches to one repeatable algebraic move before the lesson becomes more abstract.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Finding Output with $Y(j\\omega)=X(j\\omega)H(j\\omega)$",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Forming $Y(j\\omega)$ from $X(j\\omega)$ and $H(j\\omega)$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $X(j\\omega)=\\frac{1}{j\\omega+1}$ and $H(j\\omega)=\\frac{2}{j\\omega+3}$, form $Y(j\\omega)$ in product form.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the input spectrum $X(j\\omega)$ and the system response $H(j\\omega)$ are already given, so the only job is to multiply them using the frequency-domain LTI rule $Y(j\\omega)=X(j\\omega)H(j\\omega)$. Substitute the two expressions exactly as they are, with no cancellation yet.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(j\\omega)=X(j\\omega)H(j\\omega)=\\frac{1}{j\\omega+1}\\frac{2}{j\\omega+3}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the correct output spectrum in product form. Leave both factors visible so the next step can simplify the product before moving on to the inverse Fourier transform.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Finding Output with $Y(j\\omega)=X(j\\omega)H(j\\omega)$",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Simplifying the Frequency-Domain Product",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $Y(j\\omega)=\\frac{2(j\\omega+4)}{(j\\omega+1)(j\\omega+2)}\\cdot\\frac{j\\omega+1}{j\\omega+4}$, simplify the product before inversion.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the product already contains a factor that appears in both the numerator and denominator, so the job is to simplify $Y(j\\omega)$ before inversion. Start from the product exactly as formed in the previous step and keep the notation unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(j\\omega)=\\frac{2(j\\omega+4)}{(j\\omega+1)(j\\omega+2)}\\cdot\\frac{j\\omega+1}{j\\omega+4}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now cancel the shared factors $(j\\omega+1)$ and $(j\\omega+4)$. After the cancellation, the spectrum is much simpler and is ready to match a standard inverse-transform pair.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(j\\omega)=\\frac{2}{j\\omega+2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This reduced form is the correct answer for this step. Keep the output in this transform-ready shape so the next step can recover $y(t)$ without carrying the canceled factors any farther.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Finding Output with $Y(j\\omega)=X(j\\omega)H(j\\omega)$",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Inverting $Y(j\\omega)$ Using a Known Transform Pair",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $Y(j\\omega)=\\frac{2}{j\\omega+2}$, use the standard pair for $e^{-at}u(t)$ to find $y(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the single factor $j\\omega+2$ in the denominator. That matches a standard causal exponential pair, so the job here is to recognize the form and read off the time-domain signal directly.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(j\\omega)=\\frac{2}{j\\omega+2}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad y(t)=\\mathcal{F}^{-1}\\{Y(j\\omega)\\}=2e^{-2t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The constant $2$ stays outside the pair, and the value $2$ in the denominator becomes the decay rate. The inverse transform gives the output $y(t)=2e^{-2t}u(t)$ with no extra algebra.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Finding Output with $Y(j\\omega)=X(j\\omega)H(j\\omega)$",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Finding $y(t)$ from a Decomposed Frequency-Domain Product",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $Y(j\\omega)=\\frac{2}{(j\\omega+1)(j\\omega+3)}$, decompose it into standard terms and find $y(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the product of two first-order factors in the denominator. That means split $Y(j\\omega)$ into a sum of standard single-pole terms, then invert each term separately.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(j\\omega)=\\frac{2}{(j\\omega+1)(j\\omega+3)}=\\frac{A}{j\\omega+1}+\\frac{B}{j\\omega+3}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Clear the denominators to get $2=A(j\\omega+3)+B(j\\omega+1)$. Matching the poles gives $A=1$ and $B=-1$, so the spectrum becomes a difference of two standard causal-exponential pairs.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(j\\omega)=\\frac{1}{j\\omega+1}-\\frac{1}{j\\omega+3}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad y(t)=e^{-t}u(t)-e^{-3t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the output signal. The decomposition lets linearity handle the inverse transform term by term, so the final answer is just the sum of the two time-domain pieces.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
